{"id":"sh/10zixgbe","kind":"textbox","position":{"left":48,"top":158,"width":620,"height":37},"text":"For a student","style":{"fontSize":26,"typeface":"Avenir Next","color":"#D7FC70","bold":true,"alignment":"left"},"geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0},
{"id":"sh/exoj21sn","kind":"textbox","position":{"left":48,"top":203,"width":620,"height":115},"text":"Start with a concrete question and real NASA images. Practice separating what is visible from the explanation it supports. Leave with a written conclusion that can be discussed, rather than only a screenshot.","style":{"fontSize":23,"typeface":"Avenir Next","color":"#F5F6F5","alignment":"left"},"geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0},
{"id":"sh/fyh0v6t8","kind":"textbox","position":{"left":48,"top":336,"width":620,"height":37},"text":"For a teacher or astronomy club","style":{"fontSize":26,"typeface":"Avenir Next","color":"#D7FC70","bold":true,"alignment":"left"},"geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0},
{"id":"sh/sv610rah","kind":"textbox","position":{"left":48,"top":381,"width":620,"height":127},"text":"Use a guided case as a short activity. Compare explanations against the same observations, then discuss how dates, wavelengths or unreliable pixels shaped the conclusion. No account or installation is needed.","style":{"fontSize":23,"typeface":"Avenir Next","color":"#F5F6F5","alignment":"left"},"geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0},
{"id":"sh/twfitwb2","kind":"textbox","position":{"left":48,"top":511,"width":620,"height":37},"text":"A reason to return","style":{"fontSize":26,"typeface":"Avenir Next","color":"#D7FC70","bold":true,"alignment":"left"},"geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0},
{"id":"sh/6tojyhsb","kind":"textbox","position":{"left":48,"top":556,"width":620,"height":110},"text":"Reopen saved notes or try another target: Barnard’s Star, Epsilon Indi B, WISE 0855, RW Aurigae or R Draconis. Live search extends the workflow to your own object or coordinates.","style":{"fontSize":22.51,"typeface":"Avenir Next","color":"#F5F6F5","alignment":"left"},"geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0},
